  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1120 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nWarm and muggy conditions currently exist throughout the Mid-\nSouth this evening with scattered showers and thunderstorms along\nand north of I-40 ahead of a surface stationary front. Short-term\nmodels show that instability may hang on through the night,\nhovering around 1500- 2000 J/kg just ahead of the front.\nContinued lift over the surface front and weak upper height falls\nahead of a low-amplitude upper trough will therefore support\nadditional thunderstorm development and/or maintenance into\nFriday morning. However, weak shear values are expected to keep\nthe severe threat relatively low with strong, gusty winds\nremaining the primary severe hazard.\n\nThe weak upper troughing will gradually make its way into the Mid-\nSouth through Friday with the stationary boundary remaining\nacross southern MO east into KY. Therefore, surface dewpoints are\nexpected to remain in the upper 60s and low 70s, leading to\nafternoon MLCAPE values between 2000 - 3000 J/kg across the\nmajority of the Mid-South. CAMs then show diurnal thunderstorms\ninitiating across the region into the early evening, which are\nforecast to be higher in coverage compared to the past weeks'\nisolated to non-existent coverage. Wind shear values will be\nweak, leading to the primary severe hazard being potentially\ndamaging winds from hybrid microbursts. On the other hand, PWATs\nare forecast to surge well above 2.00\", which in combination with\nstorm motions below 20 knots could lead to localized flash\nflooding. Lastly, temperatures should be marginally cooler than\ntoday due to morning cloud cover and outflow from afternoon\nthunderstorms. Some areas could still breach 105 F heat indices,\nespecially across the MS River Delta in northwest MS and eastern\nAR.\n\nThe forecast will warm up through the weekend as the troughing\nresponsible for Friday's thunderstorms gets swallowed up by\nsynoptic ridging over the southern CONUS. Temperatures on\nSaturday will already get back up above 95 for the vast majority\nof the region with similar highs on Sunday and Monday. Isolated\nto scattered thunderstorms are possible Saturday across portions\nof north MS and West TN that may lead to local relief from the\nheat, but PoPs quickly drop to near zero Sunday, which lasts\nthrough at least the middle of next week. Therefore, dangerous\nheat is expected to return early next week with the potential for\nheat headlines as early as Sunday. Some ensembles are beginning\nto hint at a pattern change by the latter half of next week with\nnorthwesterlies aloft across MS River Valley. However, these\nmembers are still too few in number and show high uncertainty.\nSo, although there may be some signs of an end to the current\nheat wave, it is still too soon to say for sure that the bells\nwill be tolling for this ridge, and the heat, late next week.",
